    "id": "train-7016",
    "question": "At its current growth rate, a certain plant will be 80 feet tall after a year. If the plant grows at the same rate every month and is currently 20 feet tall, what's its monthly growth rate in feet?",
    "gold": "5",
    "answer": "If the plant is currently 20 feet tall, it will have grown by 80-20=<<80-20=60>>60 feet after a year.\nSince a year has 12 months, and the tree grows at the same rate every month, the tree grows at 60/12=<<60/12=5>>5 feet every month.\n#### 5"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the monthly growth rate of the plant, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the current height of the plant and the height it will be after one year.\n2. Calculate the total growth over the year.\n3. Determine the monthly growth rate by dividing the total growth by the number of months in a year.\n\nLet's start with the given information:\n- The plant will be 80 feet tall after one year.\n- The plant is currently 20 feet tall.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total growth over the year:\n\\[ \\text{Total growth} = \\text{Height after one year} - \\text{Current height} = 80 \\text{ feet} - 20 \\text{ feet} = 60 \\text{ feet} \\]\n\nNext, we determine the number of months in a year, which is 12 months. The monthly growth rate is the total growth divided by the number of months:\n\\[ \\text{Monthly growth rate} = \\frac{\\text{Total growth}}{\\text{Number of months}} = \\frac{60 \\text{ feet}}{12 \\text{ months}} = 5 \\text{ feet per month} \\]\n\nTherefore, the monthly growth rate of the plant is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
